contrib/pg_bp_osic/DESIGN

OSIC Design Notes
=================

This document describes the internal design of the OSIC (One-Stage Insert
Clock) buffer replacement algorithm implemented in pg_bp_osic.c.  It is
intended for engineers working on the code.


A. Algorithm Theory
-------------------

Each buffer has a boolean "cool" flag stored as bit 0 of a pg_atomic_uint32.

On a cache hit, the flag is cleared atomically:

    pg_atomic_fetch_and_u32(&states[local_id].flags, ~OSIC_FLAG_COOL);

This is the entire hot-path operation.  No spinlock.  No list manipulation.
Just an atomic AND that is often a no-op (the flag is already clear).

Eviction uses a two-stage clock sweep:

    Stage 1: The buffer is hot (cool == 0).
             Mark it cool with pg_atomic_fetch_or_u32.
             Advance the clock hand.

    Stage 2: The buffer is cool (cool == 1) AND has refcount == 0.
             Evict it.

A buffer must survive at least one full clock cycle before it can be
evicted, which provides basic scan resistance: a single sequential scan
cannot immediately flush the working set.

An atomic "inflight" counter (pg_atomic_uint32 in OsicControl) tracks
pages currently being loaded.  It is incremented in on_miss and
decremented in on_new_tag.  This prevents the eviction loop from
releasing more buffers than necessary.

The algorithm is inspired by the LeanStore buffer manager (Leis et al.,
ICDE 2018).


B. Partitioned Clock
--------------------

The buffer range is divided into OSIC_MAX_PARTITIONS (16) partitions.
Each partition has:

    - Its own spinlock (slock_t lock)
    - Its own clock_hand (int, wraps within partition range)
    - Its own buffer range (first_buf, nbuffers)

The number of partitions scales with pool size:

    < 256 buffers:    1 partition
    256 - 1023:       2 partitions
    1024 - 4095:      4 partitions
    4096 - 16383:     8 partitions
    >= 16384:        16 partitions

Backends choose a starting partition via:

    start_part = ((unsigned int) MyProcNumber) % npartitions;

This distributes backends across partitions so that different backends
can sweep different partitions concurrently.  The cast to unsigned
handles INVALID_PROC_NUMBER (-1).

The partition spinlock is held only during clock sweep (OsicGetVictim).
It is never held during on_hit, on_miss, or on_new_tag.


C. Cache Line Padding (False Sharing Fix)
------------------------------------------

OsicBufState is defined as:

    typedef struct OsicBufState
    {
        pg_atomic_uint32 flags;     /* bit 0 = cool */
        char pad[PG_CACHE_LINE_SIZE - sizeof(pg_atomic_uint32)];
    } OsicBufState;

PG_CACHE_LINE_SIZE is 128 bytes on most platforms (defined in
pg_config_manual.h).

Without padding, sizeof(pg_atomic_uint32) is 4 bytes, so 32 OsicBufState
entries would fit in a single 128-byte cache line.  On every on_hit call,
pg_atomic_fetch_and_u32 would invalidate the cache line for all 31
neighboring buffers.  On a system with 64 cores hitting different buffers,
this causes severe false sharing.

With padding, each buffer's flags occupy a full cache line.  An on_hit
call only invalidates its own cache line, eliminating false sharing
entirely.

Memory cost:

    4096 buffers (32 MB pool):     4096 * 128 = 512 KB
    131072 buffers (1 GB pool):    131072 * 128 = 16 MB

This is an acceptable tradeoff for many-core systems where the false
sharing penalty would otherwise dominate CPU time on the hit path.


D. Per-Partition Statistics
---------------------------

Each OsicPartition has four atomic stat counters:

    pg_atomic_uint64 stat_hits;
    pg_atomic_uint64 stat_misses;
    pg_atomic_uint64 stat_evictions;
    pg_atomic_uint64 stat_cooling_sweeps;

Having per-partition counters eliminates cross-partition atomic cache-line
bouncing on stat updates.  If all partitions shared a single set of stat
counters, every on_hit across all cores would bounce the same cache line.

Per-backend local stats are maintained in:

    static uint64 osic_local_stats[OSIC_MAX_PARTITIONS][OSIC_NUM_STATS];

The PoolStatIncrement function increments the local counter and flushes
to the shared atomic counter when the local count reaches 256 (the
POOL_STAT_FLUSH_THRESHOLD).  This reduces atomic operations on the shared
counters by a factor of 256.

The pg_stat_osic view aggregates across all partitions by summing each
partition's counters.

Limitation: on_miss stats are always attributed to partition 0 because
there is no buffer ID available at miss time to determine which partition
the page will eventually land in.


E. Victim Selection (OsicGetVictim)
------------------------------------

The victim selection proceeds in two passes:

Pass 1: Free buffer scan
.........................

Scan all partitions (round-robin from start_part) looking for buffers
where BM_TAG_VALID is false (i.e., the buffer has never been used or was
previously freed).  No clock sweep is performed, no buffers are cooled.
This prevents unnecessary cooling of working set pages when free buffers
exist scattered among tagged pages.

For each candidate: check refcount == 0, then CAS to pin the buffer.

Pass 2: Clock sweep (cool-and-evict)
.....................................

Only reached when Pass 1 finds no free buffers.

Outer loop: up to 3 * npartitions attempts across all partitions,
round-robin from start_part.

For each partition: acquire the partition spinlock, then sweep up to
2 * nbuffers entries (one cycle to cool everything, one cycle to evict).

Within each sweep step:

    1. Check BM_TAG_VALID == false: if free, try to pin and return.
    2. Check refcount != 0: if pinned, skip.
    3. Read the cool flag:
       - If hot (cool == 0): set the cool flag with pg_atomic_fetch_or_u32.
         Increment stat_cooling_sweeps.  Continue.
       - If cool (cool == 1): try to CAS-pin the buffer.  If successful,
         increment stat_evictions and return.

Release the partition spinlock after the inner sweep completes (whether
or not a victim was found in this partition).

If no victim is found after all attempts, raise an ERROR.


F. Implementation Details
-------------------------

Memory layout in DSM:

    +-------------------------------+
    | OsicControl (header)          |  MAXALIGN'd
    +-------------------------------+
    | OsicPartition[0..npartitions] |  MAXALIGN'd
    +-------------------------------+
    | OsicBufState[0..nbuffers]     |  MAXALIGN'd
    +-------------------------------+

Access macros:

    OSIC_PARTITIONS(ctl)  -- pointer to first OsicPartition
    OSIC_BUFSTATES(ctl)   -- pointer to first OsicBufState

OsicControl fields:

    nbuffers        -- total buffers in this pool
    first_buf_id    -- global buffer ID of the first buffer in this pool
    npartitions     -- number of clock partitions (1..16)
    inflight        -- pg_atomic_uint32, pages currently being loaded

OsicPartition fields:

    lock            -- slock_t, protects clock_hand during sweep
    clock_hand      -- int, current sweep position within partition range
    first_buf       -- int, first local buffer index in this partition
    nbuffers        -- int, number of buffers in this partition
    stat_hits       -- pg_atomic_uint64
    stat_misses     -- pg_atomic_uint64
    stat_evictions  -- pg_atomic_uint64
    stat_cooling_sweeps -- pg_atomic_uint64

OsicBufState fields:

    flags           -- pg_atomic_uint32, bit 0 = cool flag
    pad             -- char[PG_CACHE_LINE_SIZE - sizeof(pg_atomic_uint32)]

osic_partition_for_buf(ctl, local_id):

    Linear scan of partitions to find the one whose range contains the
    given local buffer ID.  With at most 16 partitions this scan is
    trivially fast.  It could be replaced with arithmetic if partitions
    are guaranteed to be equal-sized, but the current scheme distributes
    remainder buffers to early partitions (n = bufs_per_part + (i <
    remaining ? 1 : 0)), so partition sizes may differ by one.


G. Concurrency Model
---------------------

    on_hit:      LOCK-FREE
                 pg_atomic_fetch_and_u32 on the cool flag.
                 PoolStatIncrement on per-partition local stats.
                 This is the key advantage of OSIC.

    on_miss:     Lock-free.
                 pg_atomic_fetch_add_u32 on inflight counter.
                 PoolStatIncrement on partition 0 stats.

    on_new_tag:  Lock-free.
                 pg_atomic_write_u32 to reset cool flag.
                 pg_atomic_fetch_sub_u32 to decrement inflight.

    on_evict:    No-op.  Nothing to clean up.

    get_victim:  Partition spinlock during clock sweep only.
                 Different backends can sweep different partitions
                 concurrently.  Pass 1 (free buffer scan) is lock-free.

This is the most scalable algorithm among the buffer pool options for
many-core systems with high hit rates, because the dominant operation
(on_hit) never contends on a shared lock or counter.


H. Comparison with Other Algorithms
-------------------------------------

vs Clock (PostgreSQL default):

    OSIC adds partitioning and the dedicated cool flag, but loses the
    usage_count gradient (0-5) that the default clock sweep provides.
    A page that has been accessed 5 times has usage_count=5 in the default
    and must survive 5 sweep cycles; in OSIC it has cool=0 and must
    survive only 1 cycle.  The default clock thus gives more protection to
    frequently accessed pages.

vs ARC/CAR:

    OSIC has no ghost lists, no adaptation, and no frequency tracking
    beyond the binary cool/hot state.  It cannot detect workload phase
    changes or dynamically rebalance recency vs. frequency.  However,
    the lock-free hit path is significantly faster on many-core systems.

vs LRU:

    OSIC has clock-based scan resistance: a cool page survives one sweep
    cycle before eviction.  Pure LRU has no such resistance; a single
    sequential scan can flush the entire cache.

Sweet spot:

    Workloads where the hit rate is high (> 95%) and contention on the
    eviction spinlock is the bottleneck.  In these workloads, the cost
    of on_hit dominates total CPU time, and OSIC's lock-free hit path
    provides the largest benefit.


I. Memory Overhead
------------------

Per buffer:

    OsicBufState: PG_CACHE_LINE_SIZE bytes (128 bytes typically).
    Most of this is padding.

Per partition:

    OsicPartition: approximately 96 bytes (spinlock + clock_hand + range
    fields + 4 atomic stat counters).

Per pool:

    OsicControl: approximately 16 bytes (nbuffers + first_buf_id +
    npartitions + inflight counter).

No CDB entries, no ghost hash table, no buf_to_cdb mapping.

Total per buffer: approximately 128 bytes.  This is comparable to ARC
if you count its CDB overhead, but the padding dominates in OSIC.  The
tradeoff is memory for throughput: the padding eliminates false sharing
that would otherwise limit scalability.


